{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/design/datom.world.md && sed -n '3980,4045p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md && sed -n '1,240p' test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/design/datom.world.md && sed -n '3980,4045p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md && sed -n '1,240p' test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n| `test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj` | Demonstrates PHP/TypeScript substrate composition using lookup rewriting and a test-local `js-shaped` wrapper; not proof of this convention. |\n\nImplementation proceeds as separately verified changes:\n\n1. Supply the shared guest prelude and fixtures for entry discovery,\n   record validation, and condition transfer, using existing bundled/\n   linked prelude emitter machinery.\n2. Add entries to each supported callable constructor and builtin;\n   preserve source metadata/private code while putting binding and\n   exception boundaries under the entry. Keep existing source `:call`\n   safepoint marks on their original source activation, normally the\n   private body; generated entry/binding boilerplate remains unmarked.\n   Do not copy a body's source mark onto its entry and count both. A\n   separately proved instrumentation transformation may relocate a mark\n   only while preserving its source timing and exactly one activation.\n3. Route source-resolved calls and callable tests through the convention,\n   including supported bound methods, callable objects, recursion, and\n   nested runtime re-entry.\n4. Integrate explicit export/name projections with module publication\n   and task-local instantiation; remove test-only language-pair wrappers\n   from the new conformance paths.\n5. Pass the acceptance gates before advertising a conforming frontend\n   revision. Old body signatures must not be mistaken for new entries.\n\nA frontend is not conformant while its advertised supported callable\nkinds need pairwise wrappers. Legacy non-ANTLR frontends gain no claim\nfrom this proposal; joining later requires the same entry obligations.\nThere is no implication that current Python, PHP, TypeScript, or a host\ntarget has completed this migration.\n\n#### 8.10.11 Acceptance gates\n\nExecute real lowered source bodies, not only synthetic functions with\nthe expected slot. A harness may compose published exports and namespace\nprojections explicitly. It must not rewrite call nodes, extract foreign\n`:code`, or insert test-local language-pair wrappers to make calls work.\n\nRequired evidence:\n\n- **Local semantics:** existing suites preserve argument order, defaults,\n  required/extra arguments, keyword errors, receivers, exceptions, and\n  supported reference/static behavior.\n- **Direct calls:** each implemented frontend calls every other\n  conforming frontend through the same mechanism in both directions\n  with compatible values. Derive the test matrix from admitted\n  frontends; do not hard-code it into runtime dispatch.\n- **Higher-order calls:** pass, return, store, recurse, and call back\n  through received functions. Assert identity and captures without\n  replacement cells.\n- **Callable admission:** source data with an entry-spelled property\n  remains data; profile-constructed descriptors are admitted. Exercise\n  supported PHP string/array designators both under local resolution and\n  explicit callable export, preserving required late lookup without\n  foreign namespace guessing.\n- **Presence and binding:** distinguish missing from explicit\n  null/None/undefined; retain duplicate keywords until binding; supply\n  bound self once; evaluate receiver/spreads/defaults at source-defined\n  times and counts.\n- **Dynamic callability:** where supported, an existing instance becomes\n  eligible after its class gains a call method and ineligible after\n  deletion. Calls whose arguments change this metadata observe the\n  correct source-required lookup time without instance rewrapping.\n- **Opaque values:** round-trip foreign objects, functions, strings,\n  float64 content, and exact integers unchanged. Assert aliasing and no\n  silent rounding/null conversion. Unsupported operations yield\n  documented guest errors.\n(ns yang.cross-lang-test\n  \"The substrate claim, empirically (yang.antlr.md 8.10/8.11): both preludes\n   bottom out in `:application`, and the engine neither knows nor cares which\n   frontend minted a closure. A TypeScript function value is called from\n   PHP-lowered code and a PHP closure from TypeScript-lowered code, in one\n   VM, one run.\n\n   The seam is the lowered tree, not a prelude change. Each direction lowers\n   a program normally, then (1) extracts the foreign function value by\n   running the donor program with its `run-main` replaced so the program's\n   value IS the function (`js/make-function` cell, `php/make-function` cell),\n   and (2) binds that value to a free variable of the receiver, replacing the\n   receiver's lookup node (`php/resolve-fn` of a name; `js/global-get` of a\n   name) by that variable. Both function values are cells whose `:code` is\n   called with a receiver slot and an argument vector, so the callee needs no\n   knowledge of the caller's prelude.\n\n   What is pinned as LIMITS (documented expectations, not failures):\n   a PHP array reaching TypeScript is an opaque handle that only PHP can use;\n   PHP null (`:php/null`) is neither JS null nor undefined. The JVM lane runs\n   the AST walker only.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n    [clojure.walk :as walk]\n    [yang.php.antlr.e2e-harness :as php]\n    [yang.php.antlr.portable-harness :as php-h]\n    [yang.typescript.antlr.e2e-test :as ts]\n    [yang.typescript.antlr.render :as render]\n    [yin.vm :as vm]\n    [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]))\n\n\n(defn- app?\n  [node op]\n  (and (map? node)\n       (= :application (:type node))\n       (= {:type :variable, :name op} (:operator node))))\n\n\n(defn- find-node\n  \"The first node of `tree` satisfying `pred`, preorder.\"\n  [pred tree]\n  (let [hit (volatile! nil)]\n    (walk/prewalk (fn [n] (when (and (nil? @hit) (pred n)) (vreset! hit n)) n)\n                  tree)\n    @hit))\n\n\n(defn- var-node\n  [name]\n  {:type :variable, :name name})\n\n\n(defn- donor\n  \"The AST whose value is the function value of the donor program: the\n   `run-main` application becomes a call of its body, which is replaced by\n   the first function-making node (`make-fn`) of the program.\"\n  [ast run-main make-fn body-args]\n  (let [fn-node (find-node #(app? % make-fn) ast)]\n    (assert fn-node (str \"no \" make-fn \" node in the donor\"))\n    (walk/prewalk\n      (fn [n]\n        (if (app? n run-main)\n          {:type :application,\n           :operator {:type :lambda,\n                      :params ['%body],\n                      :body {:type :application,\n                             :operator (var-node '%body),\n                             :operands body-args}},\n           :operands [{:type :lambda,\n                       :params (if (= make-fn 'js/make-function) ['%0] []),\n                       :body fn-node}]}\n          n))\n      ast)))\n\n\n(defn- lowered-php\n  [source]\n  (let [{:keys [ast diagnostics]} (php/lower-program source)]\n    (assert (nil? diagnostics) (pr-str diagnostics))\n    ast))\n\n\n(defn- lowered-ts\n  [source]\n  (let [r (ts/lower-source source :bundled)]\n    (assert (nil? (:diagnostics r)) (pr-str (:diagnostics r)))\n    (let [{:yin/keys [batch root]} (:program r)]\n      (nth batch root))))\n\n\n(defn- bind\n  \"`receiver` with `v-name` bound to the value of `donor-ast`.\"\n  [donor-ast v-name receiver]\n  {:type :application,\n   :operator {:type :lambda, :params [v-name], :body receiver},\n   :operands [donor-ast]})\n\n\n(defn- registry\n  []\n  (php-h/host-registry))\n\n\n(defn- run\n  [ast]\n  (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm {:modules (registry)}) ast)))\n\n\n(defn ts-into-php\n  \"PHP `source` calling `ts_fn(...)`, with `ts_fn` the TS function `ts-src`\n   (a program of one arrow expression). Returns the printed text.\"\n  [ts-src source]\n  (let [fn-ast (donor (lowered-ts ts-src) 'js/run-main 'js/make-function\n                      [{:type :literal, :value :js/undefined}])\n        php-ast (walk/postwalk\n                  (fn [n]\n                    (if (app? n 'php/resolve-fn)\n                      (var-node '%tsfn)\n                      n))\n                  (lowered-php source))]\n    (php-h/render (run (bind fn-ast '%tsfn php-ast)))))\n\n\n(defn php-into-ts\n  \"TS `source` calling `phpfn(...)`, with `phpfn` the PHP closure `php-src`\n   (a program holding one closure expression). Returns the rendered value.\"\n  [php-src source]\n  (let [fn-ast (donor (lowered-php php-src) 'php/run-main 'php/make-function [])\n        ts-ast (walk/postwalk\n                 (fn [n]\n                   (if (and (app? n 'js/global-get)\n                            (= \"phpfn\" (get-in n [:operands 0 :value :js/str])))\n                     (var-node '%phpfn)\n                     n))\n                 (lowered-ts source))]\n    (render/output (run (bind fn-ast '%phpfn ts-ast)))))\n\n\n(deftest ts-closure-called-from-php-test\n  (testing \"a number crosses as a number: float in, float out\"\n    (is (= \"4\"\n           (ts-into-php \"(x, y) => x + y;\" \"<?php echo ts_fn(1.5, 2.5);\"))))\n  (testing \"the PHP caller sees the result as an ordinary value\"\n    (is (= \"yes\"\n           (ts-into-php \"(x, y) => x + y;\"\n                        \"<?php echo ts_fn(1.5, 2.5) == 4 ? 'yes' : 'no';\")))))\n\n\n(deftest php-closure-called-from-ts-test\n  (testing \"TS stores and calls the same PHP function cell it received\"\n    (is (= {:js/out [\"true 5\"], :js/uncaught nil}\n           (php-into-ts \"<?php fn($a, $b) => $a + $b;\"\n                        \"const stored = phpfn; console.log(stored === phpfn, stored(2, 3));\")))))\n\n\n(deftest limits-test\n  (testing \"a PHP array returned to TS is an opaque handle, usable in PHP\"\n    (let [src \"<?php fn($a = 7) => $a === 7 ? [1, 2, 3] : count($a);\"]\n      ;; the PHP int `count` returns reaches TS as an integer (`3n`), not a\n      ;; JS number: the two preludes keep their own number kinds\n      (is (= {:js/out [\"3n\"], :js/uncaught nil}\n             (php-into-ts src \"const h = phpfn(); console.log(phpfn(h));\")))))\n  (testing \"PHP null is neither JS null nor undefined\"\n    (let [src \"<?php fn() => null;\"]\n      (is (= {:js/out [\"false false\"], :js/uncaught nil}\n             (php-into-ts src\n                          \"const n = phpfn(); console.log(n === null, n === undefined);\"))))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
