{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '3560,3645p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; rg -n \\\"\\\\[php/resolve-fn|php/resolve-fn|functions\\\" src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc | head -30; rg -n \\\"defn lower-program|:prelude\\\" src/clj/yang/php/antlr/lower.clj | head -50; sed -n '2160,2260p' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc\"","aggregated_output":";; `entry` and `body-code` are live Yin closures, not literal AST maps.\n{:yang.call/entry entry\n :py/type :function\n :spec parameter-spec\n :defaults defaults\n :code body-code}\n```\n\nThe existing `:code` field is not the shared entry: its signature differs\nbetween preludes. It may remain private. Cross-language callers must not\ninspect it or extract it to bypass the entry.\n\nA cell-backed callable stores the entry in its cell contents. An\nimmutable callable descriptor, such as a bound method, may carry it\ndirectly in its map. The common prelude projects the entry structurally:\nread an existing sealed cell reference through `cell/get`, or inspect\nthe immutable descriptor, then obtain `:yang.call/entry`. It must not\ndispatch on `:py/type`, `:js/type`, `:php/type`, language names, or a\nregistry of languages.\n\nAn admitted callable is a runtime cell/descriptor constructed or exposed\nby the callable constructors or import/export projections of a pinned,\nconforming profile. The entry slot is a runtime descriptor field, not a\nsource property that promotes arbitrary data to a function. A Python\ndictionary, JavaScript object, or PHP array containing a source key\nspelled `:yang.call/entry` remains ordinary source data unless an explicit\nprofile-defined projection makes it a callable descriptor. Source\nproperty access must not overwrite or masquerade as this runtime slot.\nThis is an obligation of construction and source resolution, not a new\nVM type, descriptor seal, or runtime provenance/language registry. Once\nthe resolved descriptor is admitted, the shared projection is structural;\nUAST deliberately constructing a conforming descriptor opts into the\nconvention rather than changing a source language's data-map semantics.\n\nMissing entry means not callable. Constructors must install a genuine\nunary Yin closure, and supported source mutations must preserve that\ninvariant. Unary arity is a constructor/profile conformance obligation,\nnot a signature introspection operation: Yin closures are opaque and the\nVM's existing argument binding does not enforce this convention's arity.\nA key in a map grants no VM privilege; application, ownership, and\neffect checks still apply. A non-applicable entry remains an operational\nVM application defect, not a source exception caught by the shared helper.\n\nFor callable kinds whose eligibility can change, the same cell or\ndescriptor may also contain `:yang.call/eligible?`, a unary Yin predicate\non the original callable. Without it, an entry is eligible by default.\nWith it, the common callable test uses its boolean result. The predicate\nreads explicit runtime metadata/heap only; it must not mutate state,\nevaluate arguments, execute the target body, or invoke getters/user hooks.\nIt has the same constructor/arity obligations as the entry. For example,\nan instance can keep one dispatch entry while eligibility follows whether\nits class currently supplies a supported call method; changing the class\ndoes not require rewriting every instance.\n\nCall eligibility is tested at the callability-check point required by\nthe caller's source, not universally before argument evaluation. The\nentry resolves/rechecks a dynamic target at its source-required dispatch\npoint so argument side effects that change eligibility remain visible.\nStatic callable kinds need no predicate.\n\nSource callable designators, such as PHP function-name strings and\ncallable arrays where supported, are resolved under their source's rules\nbefore common invocation. They do not gain entry slots merely by being\nstrings or arrays. Exporting a designator as a first-class callable needs\nan explicit frontend projection to an admitted descriptor, retaining any\nsource-required late lookup and receiver semantics. Passing the raw\nstring or array as data leaves it unchanged; a foreign caller must not\nguess its language or search an ambient namespace to invoke it. This is\nsource resolution/projection, not a language-pair wrapper around an\nalready admitted function.\n\nCross-language passage must not allocate a replacement function cell.\nReturned functions retain their original identity, captures, defaults,\nbound receiver, properties, and static state. A receiving frontend's\ncallable test follows this common eligibility rule rather than rejecting\nforeign native tags. Foreign attribute access and type reflection remain\nprofile-owned; callability grants no general right to interpret another\nruntime's cell layout.\n\nCompiler-internal lambdas and host functions do not acquire this\nconvention merely by being applicable in Yin. A source import exposing\none must emit an entry from its declared signature and effect profile.\nIt must not guess an opaque closure's signature or bypass host-effect\nand custody rules. Internal helper calls need not construct records.\n\n#### 8.10.3 Invocation record\n42:     php.rt/functions      a cell holding a map of lower-cased name to\n1193:    ;; plain value. Table functions take a plain array or a handle; value\n1194:    ;; functions (`array-get`) dereference markers, raw ones (`slot-get`) do not.\n2101:    ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- functions\n2142:       (cell/set! php.rt/functions\n2143:                  (assoc (cell/get php.rt/functions)\n2149:       (if (nil? (get (cell/get php.rt/functions) lc))\n2150:         (do (cell/set! php.rt/functions (assoc (cell/get php.rt/functions) lc f))\n2225:    [php/resolve-fn\n2228:       (let [f (get (cell/get php.rt/functions) lc)]\n2344:    ;; syntax that calls these functions.\n2397:               (not (nil? (get (cell/get php.rt/functions) (php/lc v))))\n3721:     ;; row 22: shutdown functions, the destructor phase, then the release of\n4077:               (php/resolve-fn (php/lc v) v)\n4921:  \"The functions `php/init!` registers: PHP name to prelude definition,\n4927:  \"The lower-cased names of the functions `php/init!` registers.\"\n5074:                 (define 'php.rt/functions '(cell/new {}))\n5123:(def functions-uast\n5131:  (u/then functions-uast (u/sexp->uast '(php/init!))))\n5149:    php.rt/error-reporting php.rt/functions php.rt/globals php.rt/int-max\nrg: src/clj/yang/php/antlr/lower.clj: IO error for operation on src/clj/yang/php/antlr/lower.clj: No such file or directory (os error 2)\n     (fn [f name thunk]\n       (let [slot (php/static-slot f name)]\n         (if (= (cell/get slot) :php/undef)\n           (do (cell/set! slot (thunk)) :php/null)\n           :php/null)))]\n    [php/static-bind!\n     ;; `static $n = e;`: the persistent slot holds a marker made on first\n     ;; reach; the activation's CV is bound to it (row 17)\n     (fn [f name cv thunk]\n       (let [slot (php/static-slot f name)]\n         (do (if (= (cell/get slot) :php/undef)\n               (cell/set! slot (php/new-ref (thunk)))\n               :php/null)\n             (php/bind-marker cv (cell/get slot)))))]\n    [php/check-param-cell!\n     ;; a passed argument against its declared type, once the parameter cell\n     ;; exists: coerced in place, or `TypeError`. A reference with typed\n     ;; sources must already satisfy the type without conversion.\n     (fn [cell type fname idx pname]\n       (let [cur0 (cell/get cell)\n             marker? (= (php/slot-kind cur0) :ref-marker)\n             cur (if marker? (php/ref-value cur0) cur0)\n             srcs (if marker? (php/ref-sources cur0) [])\n             r (if (> (data/count srcs) 0)\n                 (if (php/type-exact? type cur) cur :php/undef)\n                 (php/type-accepts type cur (cell/get php.rt/strict)))]\n         (if (= r :php/undef)\n           (php/throw-error\n             \"TypeError\"\n             (data/str-concat fname \"(): Argument #\" (php/to-string idx) \" ($\" pname \") must be of type \"\n                              (php/type-str type) \", \" (php/given-name cur) \" given\"))\n           (if (= r cur) :php/null (if marker? (php/ref-set! cur0 r) (cell/set! cell r))))))]\n    [php/param-ref-typed\n     ;; the marker as `param-ref` makes it; the value it holds is checked, and\n     ;; written back coerced, as PHP coerces the caller's variable in place\n     (fn [arg type fname idx pname binding-profile]\n       (let [marker? (= (php/slot-kind arg) :ref-marker)\n             cur (if marker? (php/ref-value arg) arg)\n             srcs (if marker? (php/ref-sources arg) [])\n             r (if (> (data/count srcs) 0)\n                 (if (php/type-exact? type cur) cur :php/undef)\n                 (php/type-accepts type cur (cell/get php.rt/strict)))]\n         (if (= r :php/undef)\n           (php/throw-error\n             \"TypeError\"\n             (data/str-concat fname \"(): Argument #\" (php/to-string idx) \" ($\" pname \") must be of type \"\n                              (php/type-str type) \", \" (php/given-name cur) \" given\"))\n           (let [m (php/param-ref arg binding-profile)]\n             (do (if (= r cur) :php/null (php/ref-set! m r)) m)))))]\n    [php/param-ref\n     ;; a by-reference parameter: the caller's marker (counted by the CV), or\n     ;; a fresh one around a value that is not a variable. The marker is\n     ;; binding-authorized only by the pinned invocation profile.\n     (fn [arg binding-profile]\n       (if (= binding-profile \"sha256:171656777d777865312132fe18cbc7a057a4721844a4dc450c0800381d2223eb\")\n         (if (= (php/slot-kind arg) :ref-marker) (php/addref arg) (php/new-ref arg))\n         (php/new-ref (php/addref (php/deref arg)))))]\n    [php/too-few\n     (fn [name passed required exact]\n       (php/throw-error\n         \"ArgumentCountError\"\n         (data/str-concat \"Too few arguments to function \" name \"(), \"\n                          (integer/format passed) \" passed and \"\n                          (if exact \"exactly \" \"at least \")\n                          (integer/format required) \" expected\")))]\n    [php/resolve-fn\n     ;; the function cell of lower-cased `lc`, before any argument runs\n     (fn [lc written]\n       (let [f (get (cell/get php.rt/functions) lc)]\n         (if (nil? f)\n           (php/throw-error \"Error\"\n                            (data/str-concat \"Call to undefined function \"\n                                             written \"()\"))\n           f)))]\n    [php/call\n     (fn [f args]\n       ((get (cell/get f) :yang.call/entry)\n        (assoc (assoc (assoc (assoc {} :yang.call/callee f)\n                             :yang.call/positional args)\n                      :yang.call/named [])\n               :yang.call/raise (fn [condition] (php/raise condition)))))]\n    [php/call-with-binding-profile\n     (fn [f args named binding-profile]\n       ((get (cell/get f) :yang.call/entry)\n        (assoc (assoc (assoc (assoc (assoc {} :yang.call/callee f)\n                                    :yang.call/positional args)\n                             :yang.call/named named)\n                      :yang.call/raise (fn [condition] (php/raise condition)))\n               :yang.call/binding-profile binding-profile)))]\n    ;; the internal array pointer (row 55): an ordinal in the table's `:ptr`,\n    ;; so it travels with the value (copy, DUP, upgrade); absent means 0\n    [php/ptr-of\n     (fn [arr] (let [p (get (php/arr-table arr) :ptr)] (if (nil? p) 0 p)))]\n    [php/ptr-set\n     (fn [arr p]\n       (if (= (php/slot-kind arr) :arr-handle)\n         (let [c (get arr :php/arr)\n               st (cell/get c)]\n           (do (cell/set! c (assoc st :value (assoc (get st :value) :ptr p))) arr))\n         (assoc arr :ptr p)))]\n    [php/ptr-valid? (fn [arr p] (if (>= p 0) (< p (php/array-count arr)) false))]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
